[0,'concepts','In what year was the United Nations founded?','1945',"The UN Charter was signed in June 1945 and entered into force on 24 October 1945, establishing the organization. Its purposes include peace, cooperation and human rights.",'1815','1919','1961'],
[1,'concepts','What is the Universal Declaration of Human Rights?','A 1948 statement of rights adopted by the United Nations General Assembly','It is not itself a treaty, but it shaped the binding human rights law that followed.','A military alliance of Western states','A trade agreement on tariffs','A ruling of the International Court of Justice'],
[1,'concepts','What were NATO and the Warsaw Pact?','Opposing military alliances of the Cold War','Each bound its members to collective defence, formalising the division of Europe.','Two United Nations agencies','Trade blocs created in the 1800s','Rival colonial trading companies'],
[2,'concepts','What was the Cuban Missile Crisis of 1962?','A confrontation over Soviet nuclear missiles placed in Cuba',"The confrontation created a grave danger of nuclear war. It was followed by efforts to improve direct crisis communication between the United States and Soviet Union.",'A revolution that overthrew the Cuban government','A trade embargo agreed by Latin American states','A treaty banning all nuclear weapons'],
[1,'concepts','What happened across eastern Europe in 1989?','Communist governments fell, and the Berlin Wall was opened',"Opposition movements and political changes ended communist rule in several states. Outcomes varied: many transitions were peaceful, while Romania’s was violent; German reunification followed in 1990.",'The European Union was founded','The First World War ended','The Marshall Plan began'],
[2,'concepts','What was the Suez Crisis of 1956?','A conflict following Egypt’s nationalisation of the Suez Canal','The withdrawal of the intervening powers exposed the limits of older imperial influence.','A war over the Panama Canal','A dispute about Antarctic territory','A famine relief operation in the Sahel'],
[0,'interpretation',"Why did water power and steam power matter to industrialization?","They supplied energy to run machinery at larger scales","Factories used water and steam to drive machines beyond human or animal muscle alone. Steam expanded location and output possibilities without eliminating resource constraints.",'Factories were forbidden to employ workers','Trade with other countries stopped','Farming was abandoned entirely'],
[1,'interpretation','Why did railways change nineteenth-century economies so much?','They cut the cost and time of moving goods and people overland','Cheap overland transport widened markets, which changed where things could profitably be made.','They replaced all sea trade','They were used only by armies','They ended the use of coal'],
[1,'interpretation','Why did European states compete so intensely for colonies after 1870?','Rivalry over raw materials, markets and strategic position',"Economic and strategic interests combined with nationalism, prestige and imperial rivalry. Their relative importance varied across states, places and campaigns.",'Because the territories were uninhabited','Because the United Nations required it','Because their own populations were falling'],
[2,'interpretation',"Why did trench deadlock develop on the Western Front during the First World War?","Strong defensive firepower made successful attacks difficult and costly","Machine guns, artillery, entrenchments and logistical limits hindered advances. Tactics changed over the war, and stalemate did not mean that no breakthrough ever occurred.",'Neither side had enough soldiers','Both sides ran out of ammunition at once','Winter prevented all fighting'],
[1,'interpretation','Why did the revolutions of 1848 in Europe mostly fail in the short term?',"Divisions among reformers and military recovery by established rulers","Liberal, nationalist and social aims often conflicted. In many places rulers regained military control, although some reforms survived the defeat of revolutionary governments.",'Nobody anywhere supported reform','Every ruler abdicated at once','The revolutions were never attempted'],
[1,'interpretation','What was one major political consequence of the Second World War?','The United States and the Soviet Union emerged as dominant powers',"The United States and Soviet Union had exceptional military and political influence after the war. Other states and movements still shaped events; their choices were not wholly controlled by the superpowers.",'The end of all military alliances','The restoration of empires to their pre-war size','A century without further conflict'],
[2,'interpretation','Why did decolonisation accelerate after 1945?','Weakened imperial powers faced strengthened independence movements',"The war strained imperial resources, while nationalist movements pressed their claims. International pressures also changed; independence followed different negotiated and violent paths.",'Colonies lost interest in independence','International law abolished all borders','Colonial economies had collapsed to nothing'],
[1,'interpretation','Why is the Atlantic slave trade central to the history of the Americas?','It supplied the forced labour on which plantation economies were built','Its scale shaped populations, cultures and inequalities that long outlasted abolition.','It lasted only a single decade','It involved no European participation','It had no effect on African societies'],
[0,'interpretation',"Which argument did many women’s suffrage campaigners make around 1900?",'Campaigners argued that citizenship without the vote was incomplete',"Campaigners challenged political exclusion and organized petitions, public meetings and other actions. Their arguments, allies and methods differed among countries and movements.",'Voting had only just been invented','Governments requested the change first','No men supported the change'],
[2,'interpretation','Why did the Cold War produce so many conflicts outside Europe?',"The superpowers often supported rival sides in conflicts with local causes","US–Soviet rivalry intersected with decolonization, civil wars and regional struggles. Fear of direct escalation mattered, but local actors pursued their own goals.",'Europe had no armies left','Both sides agreed to fight only at sea','Local conflicts had no other causes'],
[1,'interpretation','What did the Marshall Plan aim to achieve beyond physical rebuilding?','Stable economies that would resist political upheaval','Recovery was understood as a security policy quite as much as an economic one.','The immediate unification of Europe','The disarmament of the United States','The founding of the Warsaw Pact'],
[2,'interpretation','Why did Meiji reformers in Japan adopt foreign models so deliberately?','To build the industrial and military strength needed to avoid foreign domination','They borrowed selectively, aiming at independence rather than imitation.','Because Japan had no earlier institutions','Because a foreign power was ruling Japan','Because they intended to abolish the state'],
[0,'reasoning',"How does an underlying cause differ from an immediate trigger of a war?","An underlying cause creates background pressures; a trigger precipitates the crisis","Both can contribute to the outcome. Distinguishing long-standing conditions from a precipitating event helps explain why a crisis escalated in that context.",'A trigger always comes first','Causes are always economic','Triggers are always accidental'],
[1,'reasoning',"Why can wartime propaganda be useful historical evidence even when its claims are misleading?","It can show the messages its producers wanted audiences to accept","Historians examine purpose, audience and context while checking factual claims separately. A message does not by itself prove the producer’s private beliefs or the audience’s response.",'It is always factually accurate','It is the only surviving source type','It was never shown to the public'],
[1,'reasoning','Why is a census a useful but limited historical source?','It counts systematically, but only in the categories the state chose to record',"Categories reflect administrative choices, and omissions or reporting errors can affect counts. Comparing censuses requires attention to methods and definitions.",'It records every private opinion','It is only ever taken in wartime','It never contains any errors'],
[2,'reasoning','A treaty text and the private letters of its negotiators disagree. How should a historian proceed?','Use both, asking what each type of document was written to do','Public and private records answer different questions, and the gap between them is itself evidence.','Discard the letters as unofficial','Discard the treaty as propaganda','Assume the earlier document is correct'],
[1,'reasoning',"Why can a single-cause explanation of a revolution be misleading?","It may overlook several pressures interacting over time","An explanation should test how political, social, economic and contingent factors interacted. Giving one factor priority requires evidence; it does not justify ignoring relevant alternatives.",'Revolutions have no causes at all','Only economic causes are real','Historians may not generalise'],
[0,'reasoning','What does it mean to say a historical event was contingent?','It could have turned out differently','Recognising contingency guards against reading the outcome back into the causes.','It was certain to happen','It had no consequences','It was recorded only much later'],
[2,'reasoning','Why do national histories of the same war often differ?',"They may select different evidence and emphasize different national experiences","Archives, experiences and questions can shape historical accounts. Historians within one country can disagree, and national framing is not automatically falsification.",'Only one country keeps records','Wars have no shared facts','Historians never read foreign scholarship'],
[1,'reasoning','Why can economic statistics from the 1800s be hard to compare between countries?','States collected different categories using different methods','Comparability has to be argued for rather than assumed when the definitions differ.','No country kept any records','All figures were deliberately falsified','Currency did not yet exist'],
[0,'reasoning','An event is dated to the 19th century. Which years does that span?','1801 to 1900','Centuries are counted from the year 1, so the nineteenth century opens in 1801.','1800 to 1899','1900 to 1999','1810 to 1819'],
[2,'reasoning','Why is the label “the Industrial Revolution” sometimes questioned?','The change was gradual and uneven rather than a single sudden break','A label compresses decades of varied regional change into one dramatic word.','No industry existed in the period','It happened in only one town','Historians deny that machines were used'],
[1,'reasoning','Why do photographs need the same critical reading as written sources?','They are framed, selected and sometimes staged',"Choices of framing, timing, staging and later selection affect what a photograph conveys. Captions and other evidence help establish its context.",'They cannot be dated','They are always taken by governments','They can only show landscapes'],
[2,'reasoning','Why does the survival of a diary tell a historian something before it is even read?',"Its creation and preservation required access to writing and opportunities for survival","A diary could be written or dictated, then preserved by its author, others or chance. Survival is uneven and does not guarantee truthfulness or a representative account.",'Diaries are always truthful','Diaries were required by law','Only soldiers ever kept diaries']
