[0,'interpretation','Why is “early modern” a contested period label?','Its dates and its applicability differ greatly between world regions',"Historians debate whether the label captures changes in different regions and where to place its boundaries. It should not be applied worldwide without explanation.",'No documents survive from the period','It refers only to the twentieth century','Historians agree on its exact boundaries'],
[1,'interpretation','What does calling the present the “Anthropocene” propose for historians?','That human activity has become a force operating at geological scale',"The term highlights major human effects on Earth systems and connects human history with longer timescales. It is not a formal geological epoch; the formalization proposal was rejected in 2024.",'That human history began very recently','That climate cannot be studied historically','That geology should replace history'],
[2,'interpretation','What does distinguishing the event from the structure highlight in Braudel’s work?','That different historical processes move at very different speeds',"Braudel distinguished short events, medium-term movements and enduring structures. These timescales interact; the distinction does not mean that structures never change.",'That events are unimportant','That structures never change at all','That chronology should be abandoned'],
[0,'interpretation',"How can heritage and historical study differ in emphasis?","Heritage emphasizes valued inheritances from the past; historical study emphasizes critical investigation","These activities overlap. Heritage work can include critical research, and historical scholarship can influence present identities and decisions.",'Heritage concerns only buildings','History is always written by governments','Heritage is the older of the two'],
[1,'reasoning','What is an archival silence?',"An absence or underrepresentation in the record caused by how information is created, preserved or made available","Gaps may arise intentionally or unintentionally. Investigating their causes can be informative, but a gap alone does not establish exactly what happened.",'A rule requiring quiet in reading rooms',"A document’s standard page size",'A period that had no historians'],
[2,'reasoning',"Why is an absence of surviving evidence not automatically proof that an event never occurred?",'Records are lost and were never complete, so an absence may reflect survival rather than fact',"An argument from absence is stronger when evidence would be expected to have been created, preserved and found if the event had occurred.",'Because all evidence eventually survives','Because historians may never argue negatively','Because absences cannot be described'],
[1,'reasoning',"Within a stated causal model, how do necessary and sufficient conditions differ?","A necessary condition is required for the outcome; a sufficient condition guarantees it within the model","If the outcome occurs, a necessary condition must hold. If a sufficient condition holds, the outcome follows. A condition can be both, either or neither; applying these ideas to history needs evidence and clear assumptions.",'They are interchangeable terms','A sufficient cause always comes earlier','Only necessary causes can be evidenced'],
[2,'reasoning','Why is applying a modern category such as “nation” to a distant past risky?','The category may not match how contemporaries actually organised their loyalties',"Define the category and justify its fit with the evidence. Modern analytical terms can be useful, but should not silently impose identities or assumptions on past people.",'Old societies had no politics','Modern categories cannot be defined','Translation makes all comparison impossible'],
[1,'reasoning','Why should quantitative claims about pre-modern populations carry stated uncertainty?','The underlying records are partial and were made for other purposes',"A point estimate can be useful, but its precision must reflect source quality, assumptions and uncertainty. Ranges or sensitivity checks can show how conclusions depend on those assumptions.",'Because numbers cannot describe the past','Because population never changed','Because arithmetic was unknown then'],
[0,'reasoning','Why do historians cite primary sources precisely?','So other scholars can find the same evidence and test the interpretation',"Precise references make the evidence and reasoning traceable. Citation alone does not establish that a source is reliable or has been interpreted correctly.",'To lengthen the bibliography','Because archives charge by the citation','To prove the author visited the archive'],
[2,'reasoning','What problem does relying on translated sources introduce for analysis?','Key terms may not carry the same conceptual range in the target language','A translator’s choice can quietly settle the very question at issue.','Translations are never dated','Translated texts cannot be cited','Only original manuscripts contain facts'],
[1,'reasoning','Why is a well-chosen comparison useful even between dissimilar cases?',"Differences can help assess which explanations fit the cases","A comparison may strengthen or challenge an explanation, but other differences and context must be considered before making a causal claim.",'Because all societies are essentially alike','Because comparison replaces evidence','Because it removes the need for chronology'],
[2,'reasoning','What is the risk of characterising a period from a single exceptional case?','The case may have been preserved precisely because it was unusual',"An unusual case may attract more attention or documentation than ordinary experience. Check how representative it is before generalizing to a whole period.",'Exceptional cases cannot be dated','A single case is always representative','Historians may not study individuals'],
[0,'reasoning','What does it mean to triangulate historical evidence?','To test a claim against several independent kinds of source',"Three copies of one report do not provide three independent confirmations of its claims. Comparing sources requires checking their relationships and origins.",'To use exactly three documents','To measure distances on old maps','To arrange evidence in chronological order'],
[1,'reasoning','Why does the intended audience of a source matter to its interpretation?','What a writer expected readers to know or accept shapes what is stated and what is left out',"A confidential report and a public proclamation may select and frame information differently. Neither is automatically truthful or misleading because of its audience alone.",'Audiences never affect content','Only anonymous sources are reliable','Audience matters only for printed texts'],
[2,'reasoning','Why can a well-supported historical interpretation still be revised later?','New evidence and new questions change what the surviving record can show',"Revision can reflect new discoveries or questions, or correction of an earlier error. It does not necessarily mean the earlier work was careless or worthless.",'Because the facts themselves change','Because historians dislike agreement','Because older scholarship is never read']
